    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm having a major issue with my dark chocolate batches. i'm seeing this weird white streaking/dusty look appearing on the surface of the bars after they set. it's like a bloom. can you explain why this is happening from a chemistry standpoint and how i can fix my tempering process to stop it?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "That white streaking you're observing is fat bloom, caused by cocoa butter molecules migrating to the surface and recrystallizing into soft crystals when they migrate or when the chocolate cools too slowly. To fix this, ensure your tempering process strictly follows a three-temperature cycle to form stable beta-5 crystals so the fat locks in place correctly during setting. Simply check that your chocolate is at roughly 88-92% cocoa and never touches water or steam after tempering, as moisture can also trigger blooming.",
